<InfoPage
  title="Editorial policy"
  eyebrow="How the brief is made"
  lede="Our standard is straightforward: make the source visible, make the meaning clear, and keep reporting separate from promotion."
>
  <h2>Source selection</h2>
  <p>We monitor government health agencies, medical and scientific publishers, research institutions, and established health newsrooms. A source must provide enough substance to support the claims in our brief. Thin or inaccessible source material is reserved for shorter signals or left unpublished.</p>

  <h2>Assisted drafting and human review</h2>
  <p>Software helps us collect feeds, remove duplicates, rank stories, retrieve source material, and prepare drafts. Language models may assist with organization and plain-language drafting. No automated draft is public by default.</p>
  <p>Before publication, the editorial desk checks the headline, factual claims, dates, source links, context, commercial disclosure, and the distinction between association and causation.</p>

  <h2>Health information</h2>
  <p>Agewell Brief reports on health research and public information. It does not diagnose conditions or replace care from a qualified professional. We use specific health notes where the maturity of the evidence or the stakes of a claim make that context useful.</p>

  <h2>Commercial independence</h2>
  <p>Affiliate relationships do not determine which stories enter the editorial queue. Commercial links are disclosed, visually separated from reporting, and omitted from our syndication feed. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.</p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>When a published brief contains a meaningful error, we correct the article and add an updated timestamp. To flag a possible error, send the article URL and the specific concern through our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
</InfoPage>
